# Time and Space Complexity

## DSA Phase 1

Time and Space Complexity are fundamental concepts in Data Structures and Algorithms.

Before solving DSA problems, it is important to understand not only **whether a program works**, but also:

* How much time it takes as the input grows
* How much extra memory it uses
* How the algorithm behaves for large inputs
* Which solution is more efficient when multiple solutions exist

This README covers the Time and Space Complexity concepts required for **Phase 1 of my DSA roadmap**, with Java examples and explanations.

---

# 1. What is Time Complexity?

**Time Complexity** describes how the running time of an algorithm grows as the input size `n` increases.

We usually express it using **Big-O notation**.

We are not measuring the exact number of seconds.

Instead, we are interested in the **growth of the algorithm**.

For example:

```java
static void printNumbers(int n) {
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        System.out.println(i);
    }
}
```

If `n = 10`, the loop runs 10 times.

If `n = 100`, it runs 100 times.

If `n = 1000`, it runs 1000 times.

Therefore:

```text
Time Complexity = O(n)
```

---

# 2. What is Space Complexity?

**Space Complexity** describes how much memory an algorithm needs as the input size grows.

For basic interview analysis, we are especially interested in the **extra/auxiliary space used by the algorithm**.

Example:

```java
static void printNumbers(int n) {
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        System.out.println(i);
    }
}
```

Only a fixed number of variables are being used:

```text
i
n
```

The amount of extra memory does not grow with `n`.

Therefore:

```text
Space Complexity = O(1)
```

---

# 3. Big-O Notation

Big-O describes the upper growth rate of an algorithm.

The most common complexities to recognize are:

| Complexity   | Name         | General idea                      |
| ------------ | ------------ | --------------------------------- |
| `O(1)`       | Constant     | Does not grow with input          |
| `O(log n)`   | Logarithmic  | Input is repeatedly divided       |
| `O(n)`       | Linear       | Work grows directly with input    |
| `O(n log n)` | Linearithmic | `n` work combined with `log n`    |
| `O(n²)`      | Quadratic    | Two nested `n`-sized loops        |
| `O(2ⁿ)`      | Exponential  | Recursive branching grows rapidly |

A useful order to remember:

```text
O(1)
↓
O(log n)
↓
O(n)
↓
O(n log n)
↓
O(n²)
↓
O(2ⁿ)
```

Generally, complexities toward the top scale better for large inputs.

---

# 4. O(1) — Constant Time

An algorithm is `O(1)` when the amount of work does not depend on the input size.

Example:

```java
static int getFirst(int[] arr) {
    return arr[0];
}
```

Whether the array contains:

```text
10 elements
100 elements
1,000 elements
1,000,000 elements
```

we still access only one element.

Therefore:

```text
Time = O(1)
Space = O(1)
```

---

# 5. O(n) — Linear Time

A single loop that processes all `n` elements is usually `O(n)`.

Example:

```java
static void printNumbers(int n) {
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        System.out.println(i);
    }
}
```

The loop executes `n` times.

Therefore:

```text
Time = O(n)
Space = O(1)
```

### Why is space O(1)?

The loop does not create `n` new variables or store `n` new values.

It only uses a fixed amount of extra memory.

---

# 6. O(n²) — Quadratic Time

Two nested loops that each depend on `n` usually result in `O(n²)`.

Example:

```java
static void printPairs(int n) {
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
            System.out.println(i + " " + j);
        }
    }
}
```

The outer loop runs:

```text
n times
```

For every outer-loop iteration, the inner loop runs:

```text
n times
```

Therefore:

```text
n × n = n²
```

So:

```text
Time = O(n²)
Space = O(1)
```

### Important

Nested loops do **not automatically mean O(n²)**.

You have to check how each loop changes.

For example, if the inner loop doubles a variable, the complexity can become `O(n log n)`.

---

# 7. Separate Loops

Consider:

```java
static void fun(int n) {

    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        System.out.println(i);
    }

    for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
        System.out.println(j);
    }
}
```

The first loop:

```text
O(n)
```

The second loop:

```text
O(n)
```

Because they run one after another:

```text
O(n) + O(n)
= O(2n)
```

We drop the constant:

```text
O(n)
```

Therefore:

```text
Time = O(n)
Space = O(1)
```

### Remember

```text
Nested loops → usually multiply

n × n = O(n²)

Separate loops → add

n + n = O(n)
```

---

# 8. O(log n) — Logarithmic Time

When the input is repeatedly divided, the complexity is often `O(log n)`.

Example:

```java
static void fun(int n) {
    int i = 1;

    while (i < n) {
        System.out.println(i);
        i = i * 2;
    }
}
```

The value of `i` changes like:

```text
1
2
4
8
16
32
64
...
```

It doubles every iteration.

Therefore, the number of iterations grows logarithmically.

```text
Time = O(log n)
Space = O(1)
```

### Easy rule

If you see:

```java
i = i * 2;
```

or:

```java
i = i / 2;
```

think:

```text
O(log n)
```

---

# 9. O(n log n)

Now combine a linear loop with a logarithmic loop.

```java
static void fun(int n) {

    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {

        int j = 1;

        while (j < n) {
            System.out.println(i + " " + j);
            j = j * 2;
        }
    }
}
```

Outer loop:

```text
O(n)
```

Inner loop:

```text
O(log n)
```

Because the loops are nested:

```text
O(n) × O(log n)
```

Therefore:

```text
Time = O(n log n)
Space = O(1)
```

This complexity becomes important later when studying algorithms such as **Merge Sort**.

---

# 10. Space Complexity of Loops

A common mistake is thinking:

> "If a loop runs `n` times, space must be O(n)."

That is not necessarily true.

Example:

```java
static void fun(int n) {
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        System.out.println(i);
    }
}
```

The loop runs `n` times, so:

```text
Time = O(n)
```

But it does not store all `n` values.

It only uses `i`.

Therefore:

```text
Space = O(1)
```

### Key idea

**Time asks: How much work?**

**Space asks: How much extra memory?**

---

# 11. Recursion

Recursion happens when a function calls itself.

Example:

```java
static void fun(int n) {

    if (n == 0) {
        return;
    }

    System.out.println(n);

    fun(n - 1);
}
```

The calls look like:

```text
fun(5)
 ↓
fun(4)
 ↓
fun(3)
 ↓
fun(2)
 ↓
fun(1)
 ↓
fun(0)
```

There are approximately `n` function calls.

Therefore:

```text
Time = O(n)
```

But the recursive calls must remain on the call stack until the recursion finishes.

Therefore:

```text
Space = O(n)
```

### Important rule

For simple recursion:

```text
Number of calls → Time

Maximum recursive depth → Space
```

---

# 12. Recursion vs Iteration

Compare these two approaches.

## Iterative

```java
static void printNumbers(int n) {

    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        System.out.println(i);
    }
}
```

Complexity:

```text
Time = O(n)
Space = O(1)
```

## Recursive

```java
static void printNumbers(int n) {

    if (n == 0) {
        return;
    }

    System.out.println(n);
    printNumbers(n - 1);
}
```

Complexity:

```text
Time = O(n)
Space = O(n)
```

Both take linear time, but recursion uses additional call-stack memory.

---

# 13. Fibonacci — Iterative

An iterative Fibonacci solution:

```java
static int fibonacci(int n) {

    int a = 0;
    int b = 1;

    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        int temp = a + b;
        a = b;
        b = temp;
    }

    return a;
}
```

The loop executes `n` times.

Therefore:

```text
Time = O(n)
Space = O(1)
```

Only a few variables are maintained.

---

# 14. Fibonacci — Recursive

A basic recursive Fibonacci solution:

```java
static int fibonacci(int n) {

    if (n <= 1) {
        return n;
    }

    return fibonacci(n - 1) + fibonacci(n - 2);
}
```

The function creates two recursive branches:

```text
                 fib(n)
                /      \
           fib(n-1)   fib(n-2)
            /   \       /   \
          ...   ...   ...   ...
```

The number of calls grows very quickly.

For the standard naive recursive Fibonacci implementation:

```text
Time = O(2^n)
Space = O(n)
```

### Why space is O(n)?

The deepest chain of recursive calls can reach approximately `n`.

For example:

```text
fib(5)
 ↓
fib(4)
 ↓
fib(3)
 ↓
fib(2)
 ↓
fib(1)
```

The call stack grows with `n`.

### Important comparison

| Fibonacci       |     Time |  Space |
| --------------- | -------: | -----: |
| Iterative       |   `O(n)` | `O(1)` |
| Basic Recursive | `O(2^n)` | `O(n)` |

This is an important example of why an algorithm can be correct but still inefficient.

