# 15. Binary Search

Binary Search works on a **sorted array**.

Example:

```java
static int binarySearch(int[] arr, int target) {

    int start = 0;
    int end = arr.length - 1;

    while (start <= end) {

        int mid = (start + end) / 2;

        if (arr[mid] == target) {
            return mid;
        }
        else if (arr[mid] < target) {
            start = mid + 1;
        }
        else {
            end = mid - 1;
        }
    }

    return -1;
}
```

The search space is approximately halved after each iteration.

Example:

```text
n
↓
n/2
↓
n/4
↓
n/8
↓
...
↓
1
```

Therefore:

```text
Time = O(log n)
```

Only a few variables are used:

```text
start
end
mid
```

Therefore:

```text
Space = O(1)
```

### Final Binary Search Complexity

```text
Time = O(log n)
Space = O(1)
```

---

# 16. Linear Search vs Binary Search

## Linear Search

```java
static int linearSearch(int[] arr, int target) {

    for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {

        if (arr[i] == target) {
            return i;
        }
    }

    return -1;
}
```

Worst-case complexity:

```text
Time = O(n)
Space = O(1)
```

## Binary Search

```text
Time = O(log n)
Space = O(1)
```

### Comparison

| Algorithm     | Requirement          |       Time |  Space |
| ------------- | -------------------- | ---------: | -----: |
| Linear Search | No sorting required  |     `O(n)` | `O(1)` |
| Binary Search | Array must be sorted | `O(log n)` | `O(1)` |

