# 18. Common Patterns to Recognize

## Pattern 1: One loop

```java
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    // work
}
```

```text
Time = O(n)
```

---

## Pattern 2: Two nested loops

```java
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
        // work
    }
}
```

```text
Time = O(n²)
```

---

## Pattern 3: Separate loops

```java
for (...) {
    // O(n)
}

for (...) {
    // O(n)
}
```

```text
O(n) + O(n)
= O(2n)
= O(n)
```

---

## Pattern 4: Doubling

```java
int i = 1;

while (i < n) {
    i = i * 2;
}
```

```text
Time = O(log n)
```

---

## Pattern 5: Nested linear + logarithmic

```java
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {

    int j = 1;

    while (j < n) {
        j = j * 2;
    }
}
```

```text
O(n) × O(log n)
= O(n log n)
```

---

## Pattern 6: Simple recursion

```java
fun(n) {
    fun(n - 1);
}
```

```text
Time = O(n)
Space = O(n)
```

---

## Pattern 7: Two recursive branches

```java
fun(n) {
    fun(n - 1);
    fun(n - 2);
}
```

For the standard Fibonacci-style recursion:

```text
Time = O(2^n)
```

---

# 19. How to Analyze Time Complexity

When you see a new piece of code, follow these steps.

### Step 1: Find the main operation

Ask:

> What work is being repeated?

For example:

```java
System.out.println(i);
```

---

### Step 2: Count how many times it runs

If a loop runs `n` times:

```text
O(n)
```

If nested loops each run `n` times:

```text
O(n²)
```

---

### Step 3: Check whether loops are nested or separate

Nested:

```text
multiply
```

Separate:

```text
add
```

---

### Step 4: Look for doubling or halving

If you see:

```java
i = i * 2;
```

or:

```java
n = n / 2;
```

think:

```text
O(log n)
```

---

### Step 5: Check recursion

Ask:

1. How many recursive calls are made?
2. How deep can the recursion go?
3. Are there multiple branches?

---

### Step 6: Simplify

Examples:

```text
O(2n) → O(n)

O(5n + 10) → O(n)

O(n² + n) → O(n²)

O(n³ + n² + n) → O(n³)
```

We focus on the dominant growth.

---

# 20. How to Analyze Space Complexity

Ask:

> What extra memory grows as the input grows?

### Fixed variables

```java
int a;
int b;
int c;
```

```text
Space = O(1)
```

### Array of size n

```java
int[] arr = new int[n];
```

```text
Space = O(n)
```

### Recursive call stack

```java
fun(n - 1);
```

with depth `n`:

```text
Space = O(n)
```

